## Module 4 – Battery Energy Storage System (BESS)

### Objective
Develop a simulated Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) that responds dynamically to available solar generation while introducing embedded control logic and time-based system updates.

### Features
- Simulated Battery State of Charge (SOC)
- Initial battery charge set to 50%
- Battery charges when sufficient solar energy is available
- Battery discharges during low solar conditions
- Battery SOC limited between 0% and 100%
- Battery updates once per second using `millis()` for non-blocking timing

### Concepts Learned
- State variables
- State of Charge (SOC)
- Conditional logic (`if` statements)
- Non-blocking programming with `millis()`
- Embedded system scheduling
- Battery simulation
- Control algorithms
- Real-time system behavior

### Engineering Decisions
Instead of using fixed delays (`delay()`), the battery is updated using a non-blocking timer based on `millis()`. This allows multiple subsystems (battery management, display updates, sensors, and future Wi-Fi communication) to operate simultaneously without freezing the controller.

### Skills Demonstrated
- Embedded C++
- Battery management concepts
- Real-time firmware design
- Algorithm development
- State machine fundamentals
- Embedded timing techniques
